ok down to the third book  we have dr hirsch's new ebook on  information retrieval the fourth edition  so if you click on available online  there  you will get prompted for the library's  remote access login  you use your credentials authenticate  with duo as we've already discussed  and then you're going to come to this  particular publishers page for the book  i have to tell you ebook options are the  most confusing thing  i can't possibly show you all of them  they vary by publisher from  unlimited download by unlimited users  like this book  to ones where you can view only by a  single user at a time  and so i would really encourage you to  look at the limits and options on each  book as you  encounter it so that you can decide what  you need  from it and figure out what your best  approach will be  this particular book lets you download  the entire book in pdf  or an epub which can be used by your  reader devices  or you can go for the pdf of a  particular chapter that you need  those most limited books some of them  the only way to save  content from them is to print or save  selected pages or chapters to pdf  and some of them may limit the number of  pages in a session  or the number of pages total that we can  use from a book and that's why i say  please do explore the options on the  interface for the particular book that  you're looking at  in addition to the library catalog with  the e-books as i've just mentioned we  also have  a great number of online databases and  resources  well beyond that selection of popular  resources on the homepage we showed you  earlier in the video  these are helpful when you're looking  for information but you're not  having a particular citation in mind and  these will cover both bibliographic  citations and abstracts such as pubmed  that we already looked at or scopus  which is a multi-disciplinary citation  and abstract database  but also databases with facts or  clinical summaries such as drug  information and lexicomp o